A matrix is symmetric when \(a_{ij} = a_{ji}\) for all \(i, j\). Comparing the pairs of entries placed symmetrically about the main diagonal,

\(a_{13} = a_{31}\) gives \(a = -4\);

\(a_{21} = a_{12}\) gives \(b = \frac{3}{5}\);

\(a_{32} = a_{23}\) gives \(c = -7\).

\(\therefore a = -4\), \(b = \frac{3}{5}\), \(c = -7\)

A matrix is skew-symmetric when \(a_{ij} = -a_{ji}\) for all \(i, j\) (its diagonal entries are then \(0\), as given here). Comparing the entries placed symmetrically about the main diagonal,

\(a_{13} = -a_{31}\) gives \(x = -\frac{3}{2}\);

\(a_{21} = -a_{12}\) gives \(y = -(-5i) = 5i\);

\(a_{23} = -a_{32}\) gives \(z = -(-\sqrt{2}) = \sqrt{2}\).

\(\therefore x = -\frac{3}{2}\), \(y = 5i\), \(z = \sqrt{2}\)
